The City Council is developing and supporting the development of user-saving services throughout its region, in particular for as many neighbourhoods as possible, as part of the "10-Minute City" approach:

o At least one carsharing/pooling station;

o Libraries and digital public spaces, and easy access for all to cultural and digital content;

o Neighbourhood centres for digital access;

o Equipment loans: loan centres, fablabs, etc. (see waste chapter);

o Coworking and sharing spaces:

To meet these objectives, the Brussels City Council will entrust the Assembly with 3 main tasks:

o On the basis of the results of the participatory process organised by the City Council (2021 - 2022), complete the Climate Plan's roadmap concerning the region's objectives;

o Monitoring and evaluating the Climate Plan (monitoring, governance, analysis and recommendation reports);

o Unite  as  many  players  as  possible  around  climate  objectives  and  lead  this network.

According to the UNFCCC, COP 17 th , the main objectives of these action plan are 2 :

- a)  To  reduce  vulnerability  to  the  impacts  of  climate  change,  by  building  adaptive capacity and resilience;
- b) To facilitate the integration of climate change adaptation, in a coherent manner, into relevant new and existing policies, programmes and activities, in particular, development planning processes and strategies, within all relevant sectors and at different levels, as appropriate.

The NAP process defined by the UNFCCC comprises four main elements that can be summarized as follows:

- (A) laying the groundwork and identifying gaps;
- (B) making preparatory elements;
- (C) implementation strategies and;
- (D) reporting, monitoring and reviewing.

